WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.191
Welcome to Power Query!

2
00:00:02.191 --> 00:00:07.409
Clean data is the secret behind every great report, and this is where it happens.

3
00:00:07.409 --> 00:00:12.279
In this lesson, you will learn to find your way around the Power Query

4
00:00:12.279 --> 00:00:18.540
Editor, check the quality of every column, and profile your data before you clean it. Let's get started!

5
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.743
Here is the Power Query Editor, with the Financial Sample workbook loaded.

6
00:00:24.743 --> 00:00:26.224
First, a quick habit:

7
00:00:26.224 --> 00:00:29.557
in Query Settings, give your query a clear name.

8
00:00:29.557 --> 00:00:34.000
I call it Financial Sample, so it is easy to find later.

9
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:44.180
Click Close and Apply, and your queries appear in the Data pane, ready for the report.

10
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:01.435
Back in the editor, the Queries pane on the left lists every table you loaded.

11
00:01:01.435 --> 00:01:03.280
Let's open Financial Sample again.

12
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.992
Look at the thin bar under each column header.

13
00:01:12.992 --> 00:01:15.740
Hover over it, and you see column quality:

14
00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.549
how many values are valid, how many have errors, and how many are empty.

15
00:01:20.549 --> 00:01:21.922
Here, everything is valid.

16
00:01:21.922 --> 00:01:23.640
That is what we want!

17
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.597
On the right, Applied Steps records every change, in order:

18
00:01:31.597 --> 00:01:34.555
the source, navigation, promoted headers, and changed type.

19
00:01:34.555 --> 00:01:40.100
Click any step to go back in time and see your data at that point.

20
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.788
Now, before you clean anything, get to know your data.

21
00:02:03.788 --> 00:02:06.898
On the View tab, switch on three helpers:

22
00:02:06.898 --> 00:02:09.620
column quality, column distribution, and column profile.

23
00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:28.103
Column distribution adds a mini chart under each header, with the number of distinct and unique values.

24
00:02:28.103 --> 00:02:32.340
Segment, for example, has only five distinct values. Useful, right?

25
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.968
Column profile gives you full statistics for the selected column:

26
00:02:49.968 --> 00:02:56.071
count, errors, empty values, distinct values, and a chart of how often each value appears.

27
00:02:56.071 --> 00:03:01.360
Use it every time, and you will catch problems before your report does.

